+++ b/HEGEL_REPORT.md
@@ -0,0 +1,108 @@
+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: ndarray
+
+## 1. Scope and Approach
+
+Added 23 property-based tests (12 distinct property groups) with `hegeltest`
+0.28.2 (`hegel` crate) against ndarray's public array API, over generated
+small dynamic-dimension arrays: shapes drawn with 0–4 axes and 0–5 elements
+per axis, `i64` data (exact comparisons, no float tolerance) except where a
+property is float-specific (`mean_axis`, `f64` with a norm-scaled tolerance).
+
+ndarray is a mature, hardened crate, so per the hegel skill the tests weight
+**oracle-independent structural invariants** (round-trips, involutions, and
+element checks against the flat data `Vec` the test itself constructed and
+still holds) over differential oracles; where an oracle is used it is a naive
+loop reimplementation (triple-loop matmul, sequential folds), for which a
+disagreement would be a real bug.
+
+Existing generative tests were read first: `quickcheck` properties in
+`src/dimension/mod.rs` cover internal helpers (`can_index_slice`,
+`extended_gcd`, `arith_seq_intersect`, ...) and one `proptest` property covers
+`permute_axes`. Those were left in place (they pin internal invariants
+cheaply); the new tests target the untested public-API surface —
+reshape/transpose/slice/iterate/broadcast/concatenate/reduce/dot — and do not
+duplicate them.
+
+A maintainer AI-consent check found no `CONTRIBUTING`/governance file or
+anti-AI clause in the repository; nothing here is being upstreamed.
+
+## 2. Properties Added
+
+Shared drawing/model helpers live in `tests/hegel_support/mod.rs` (shape and
+array generators, row-major stride computation, lexicographic index
+enumeration, flat-offset model — all independent of ndarray's own indexing).
+Each property was added to the existing test file for its area:
+
+| # | Property | File | Oracle |
+|---|----------|------|--------|
+| 1 | `to_shape` to any same-size shape preserves logical element order; reshaping back restores the array exactly | `tests/reshape.rs` | independent (held data) |
+| 2 | `to_shape` with a mismatched element count returns `Err`, never panics | `tests/reshape.rs` | independent |
+| 3 | 2-D transpose: reversed shape and strides, `t[[j,i]] == a[[i,j]]` vs flat data, `t.t() == a` (involution) | `tests/array.rs` | independent |
+| 4 | n-D `reversed_axes`: reversed shape, element mapping vs flat data, involution | `tests/array.rs` | independent |
+| 5 | Per-axis slicing (`Slice::new`, negative start/end, negative steps) matches a manual index-selection model over the flat data | `tests/array.rs` | independent model of documented semantics |
+| 6 | `iter()` yields logical row-major order on both C-order arrays (== backing data) and non-contiguous reversed-axes views | `tests/iterators.rs` | independent |
+| 7 | `indexed_iter()` indices are exactly the lexicographic enumeration and values match manual flat offsets | `tests/iterators.rs` | independent |
+| 8 | `broadcast` to a compatible shape repeats elements per the index-mapping model; incompatible target (existing axis ≥ 2 changed) returns `None` | `tests/broadcast.rs` | independent |
+| 9 | `&a + &b` co-broadcasting: result shape is the common shape and every element equals `a[map_a(idx)] + b[map_b(idx)]` | `tests/broadcast.rs` | independent |
+| 10 | `concatenate`/`stack` along a drawn axis: result shape and every element vs the source data model; mismatched non-concat axis is `Err`, never a panic | `tests/stacking.rs` | independent |
+| 11 | In-place `append` matches the concatenation model (append does internal layout-shuffling, so it is covered separately); mismatch is `Err` | `tests/append.rs` | independent |
+| 12 | `sum_axis`/`product_axis`/`sum` vs naive sequential folds (i64, exact); `mean_axis` vs naive mean (f64, tolerance `4·ε·n·max|x| + MIN_POSITIVE`, `None` iff the axis is empty) | `tests/numeric.rs` | naive-fold differential |
+| 13 | 1-D `dot` vs sum-of-products; 2-D `dot` (matmul) vs a naive triple loop | `tests/oper.rs` | naive-loop differential |
+| 14 | `insert_axis`/`remove_axis` round-trip (shape, element order, exact equality); `into_dimensionality::<Ix2>` succeeds iff `ndim == 2` and round-trips through `into_dyn` | `tests/dimension.rs` | independent |
+
+Generator notes: `i64` values are full-range except where a bound protects the
+*test's own arithmetic* from inherited primitive overflow (sums, products,
+dot products); each such bound is commented as protecting the test, not a
+library contract. Slice arguments are drawn in-bounds by construction because
+out-of-bounds slicing is a documented panic. Empty axes, 0-dimensional
+arrays, and length-1 axes are all inside the generated space.
+
+## 3. Validation (Run and Reflect)
+
+- **Liveness / mutation check:** all 23 properties were individually mutated
+  (inverted assertions, or a wrong model branch) and every mutation was
+  caught with a shrunk counterexample — 23/23 across two batches.
+- **Guarded branches fire:** the `ndim == 2` branch of the
+  `into_dimensionality` property and the negative-step branch of the slice
+  model were mutated *inside the guard* and both failed, proving the guarded
+  code is actually reached (not vacuously passing).
+- **Case-count confirmation:** an atomic counter inside one test asserted
+  failure at case 1000 under `test_cases = 1000` — it failed with "reached
+  case 1000", confirming the high-count run really executes 10x cases.
+- **Exploratory 10x run:** the full property suite was run once at
+  `test_cases = 1000` (10x the committed default): all green. Committed tests
+  are back at the default count.
+- **Full suite:** `cargo test -p ndarray` (lib tests + all integration test
+  binaries, 33 result blocks) passes with 0 failures; no pre-existing test
+  was modified or broken. The `.hegel` failure database (populated only
+  during deliberate mutation runs) was removed and `.hegel/` added to
+  `.gitignore`.
+
+## 4. Findings
+
+**No bugs found.** All properties hold at the default and at 10x case counts.
+No test is pinned failing and nothing is `#[ignore]`d. This is consistent
+with ndarray being a heavily used, hardened crate; the mutation results show
+the properties are falsifiable, so the green runs are meaningful evidence for
+the core structural contracts (reshape/transpose/slice/iterate/broadcast/
+concatenate/reduce/dot) on small shapes.
+
+One behavior worth noting (verified against docs/source, not a bug): a
+negative slice step walks the `start..end` range anchored at its *back*
+(`end - 1`), not at `start` — the model test encodes and confirms exactly the
+documented `do_slice` semantics.
+
+## 5. Limitations and Future Work
+
+- Shapes are small (≤ 4–5 axes, ≤ 5 per axis) by design brief; overflow-zone
+  shapes (`isize::MAX`-scale strides) are already covered by the crate's own
+  quickcheck tests of `can_index_slice`/`max_abs_offset_check_overflow`.
+- `f32`/`f64` matmul dispatches to `matrixmultiply` (a different code path
+  than the generic i64 loop tested here); a float differential with
+  norm-scaled tolerance, and the `blas` feature path, would extend coverage.
+- Non-default features (`serde` round-trip, `rayon` parallel iterators vs
+  serial results) are untested here and are natural next differentials.
+- A stateful model test of an owned `Array` under interleaved
+  `push_row`/`push_column`/`append`/slice-assign against a `Vec<Vec<_>>`
+  model would exercise the layout-shuffling append machinery more deeply
+  than the single-append property.
